  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Provisions and Rations: Think in Person-Days",
    "description": "Model a fixed stock as consumers × daily ration × days.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat food or medicine as a stock measured in consumption-days. Start with constant ration, then let people leave or join after some days, change ration per head, combine equivalent consumer groups, and vary medicine dosage and patient count. Every stage begins by reducing the stock for days already consumed before changing the population.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach provision problems with the stock model: total stock is proportional to number of consumers × daily consumption per consumer × number of days. Cover men leaving, reinforcements arriving, ration per head changing, dosage changing while patient count changes, mixed-equivalent consumers such as men and children, and reverse questions asking the original group size or stock duration. Always subtract the days already consumed before changing the population."
  },
  "scriptId": "cr-07-provisions-rations-consumption",
